codex
Implemented a Track B generator based on the paper’s signed binary-quadratic-form relations from Sections 2.2–2.3 of the [paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0902.4670).

Deliverables:

- [gen_0902_4670.py](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0902.4670/gen_0902_4670.py)
- [selftest_report.json](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0902.4670/selftest_report.json)
- [README.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0902.4670/README.md)
- [bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0902.4670/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0902.4670/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/0902.4670/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Local results:

- All G1–G9(c) gates pass.
- Exact random-guess probability: \(2^{-30}\).
- Five adversarial attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- Reference meet-in-the-middle algorithm: 8/8, averaging 1,237,669.75 counted operations.
- Answer bound: 93 characters, 31 atoms.
- Compact route bound: 187 operations.
- Independent brute-force/property checks passed on 400 generated instances and 480 direct principality comparisons.

Step 4 remains externally blocked: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” on every bare, hinted, and placebo call. The script-owned error transcripts are preserved, and no model failures or hardening verdict were fabricated. Consequently, `submit.sh` correctly remains blocked until quota is restored and the oracle runs are repeated.
