    20	
    21	## Problem and trust model
    22	
    23	The source is Bisson and Sutherland, [*Computing the endomorphism ring of an
    24	ordinary elliptic curve over a finite field*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.4670).
    25	Section 2.2 represents ideal classes of imaginary quadratic orders by primitive
    26	positive-definite binary quadratic forms. Section 2.3 and equation (3) define a
    27	relation by choosing signs on oriented split-prime classes. An instance here
    28	gives the common discriminant and the oriented prime forms; the solver returns
    29	one normalized sign vector whose Dirichlet product is principal.
    30	
    31	This is a paper-native intermediate object, not an elliptic curve replaced by a
    32	graph. The generator first chooses a factored norm `A`, an integer `x`, and the
    33	answer signs, sets `D=x^2-4A`, and only then builds and shuffles the displayed
    34	forms. Thus G uses composition of identities. Verification combines the signed
    35	roots by exact CRT. Since `A < |D| < 3A`, a combined form `(A,B,C)` represents
    36	one exactly iff `4A+D` is a square `x^2` and `B == ±x (mod 2A)`. The checker
    37	executes those integer tests and never reads `inst["answer"]`.
    38	
    39	## Why Track B
    40	
    41	The paper is explicit about the mechanical route. Section 2.3 fixes one sign by
    42	global inversion and enumerates the other `2^(k-1)` choices; its class-group
    43	relation count uses `O(2^k + sum(log e_i))` group operations. Because this task
    44	only asks for one witness, the measured reference uses the stronger standard
    45	meet-in-the-middle search on exact reduced-form class keys. Its complexity is
    46	`O(2^(k/2) poly(log|D|))` time and memory. Over eight shipping seeds it averaged
    47	48,836.5 candidates, 64,905 form reductions, 1,237,669.75 counted exact
    48	operations, 0.981 seconds mean and 1.664 seconds maximum in the final self-test,
    49	and solved 8/8.
    50	
    51	An efficient specialized route therefore exists and is not hidden: multiply
    52	the norms, notice that `4A+D` is a square, take its exact integer square root,
    53	and compare that root with each oriented residue. It costs 178 counted exact
    54	operations on the shipping instance. The benchmark tests whether a no-tool
    55	solver finds this invariant; executing tens of thousands of reduced-form tests
    56	and roughly 1.24 million exact operations in context is not realistic.
    57	
    58	For the full endomorphism-ring problem, Algorithm `Certify` obtains relations
    59	with `FindRelation`, and Algorithm `Verify` checks them by isogeny walks.
    60	Proposition 6 gives heuristic subexponential cost for `FindRelation`,
    61	Proposition 9 does the same for verification, and Corollary 8 bounds certificate
    62	size. Small conductor primes are an easy regime handled by isogeny climbing in
    63	Algorithm 1, lines 3--4; Section 3.3 says Algorithm 2 can be much faster when
    64	`u << v`. For this relation family, small arity is plainly easy because Section
    65	2.3 enumerates the signs, so the five-form setting is only a demo and shipping
    66	uses 31 forms. Those facts rule out pretending that this constructed
    67	distribution is a Track-A endomorphism-ring benchmark.
    68	
    69	## Worked demo
    70	
    71	For `make_instance(seed=0, n=5, prime_bits=7)`, `render(inst)` is:
    72	
    73	```text
    74	Find a principal signed relation among oriented binary quadratic forms.
    75	
    76	A binary quadratic form is Q(X,Y)=a X^2+b XY+c Y^2.  Its discriminant is
    77	D=b^2-4ac.  All forms below are primitive positive-definite forms with the same
    78	negative discriminant
    79	
    80	  D = -21978630539.
    81	
    82	Row i gives the oriented prime form Q_i=(ell_i,b_i,c_i).  Choose one sign
    83	epsilon_i in {+1,-1} for every row.  Let
    84	
    85	  A = product_i ell_i.
    86	
    87	There is a unique odd B in the half-open interval 0 <= B < 2A satisfying
    88	
    89	  B = epsilon_i*b_i (mod 2*ell_i)  for every i.
    90	
    91	(The ell_i are distinct odd primes, so these congruences are compatible and
    92	determine B.)  Put C=(B^2-D)/(4A).  Your signs are valid exactly when the
    93	combined form Q=(A,B,C) is principal.  Here "principal" means that there exist
    94	integers r,s with A*r^2+B*r*s+C*s^2=1.
    95	
    96	Because negating every sign gives the inverse of the same relation, fix
    97	epsilon_0=+1.  Row indices are 0-based, order matters only through these row
    98	indices, repetitions are not allowed, and all congruence intervals above use
    99	the stated inclusive/exclusive endpoints.
   100	
   101	The 5 rows are:
   102	  0: ell=107, b=147, c=51351991
   103	  1: ell=109, b=29, c=50409705
   104	  2: ell=103, b=87, c=53346209
   105	  3: ell=97, b=193, c=56646051
   106	  4: ell=79, b=143, c=69552693
   107	
   108	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as a JSON list of exactly
   109	5 integers, each 1 or -1, with the first entry 1.
   110	Format example: <answer>[1,-1,1,-1,1]</answer>
   111	Output nothing else inside the tags.
   112	```
   113	
   114	The answer is `<answer>[1,1,-1,1,1]</answer>`, and
   115	`verify(inst, [1,1,-1,1,1]) == (True, "ok")`. Flipping the second sign gives
   116	`verify(inst, [1,-1,-1,1,1]) == (False, "the signed combined form is not
   117	principal")`. A person can solve this smallest instance by checking its 16
   118	normalized sign vectors on paper; it is intentionally illustrative.
